We start from Ludwig’s local model for polytope approximation Ludwig (1999) . Write m = d − 1 m=d-1 . The local problem is to approximate the paraboloid q ⁡ ( x ) = | x | 2 q(x)=|x|^{2} on I = [ 0 , 1 ] m I=[0,1]^{m} by a convex piecewise-affine function h h with at most N N vertices. The error ∫ I | h − q | ​ 𝑑 x \int_{I}|h-q|\,dx is then the volume between the two graphs. Our bound compares the error in this unrestricted vertex case with the error where vertices are fixed to the values of q q resulting in an inscribed approximation. The main step is to construct, on each simplex of the triangulation defined by the N N points, a sign that annihilates every affine height correction in h h . This construction can be viewed as an application of the dual formula for L 1 L^{1} approximation. We express the two local errors as e N ldel ​ ( I ) := inf h ∫ I | h − q | ​ 𝑑 x , e N del ​ ( I ) := inf 𝒯 ∫ I ( ℐ 𝒯 ​ q − q ) ​ 𝑑 x . e_{N}^{\ldel}(I):=\inf_{h}\int_{I}|h-q|\,dx,\qquad e_{N}^{\operatorname{del}}(I):=\inf_{\mathcal{T}}\int_{I}(\mathcal{I}_{\mathcal{T}}q-q)\,dx. In the first infimum, we count the vertices of the subdivision into maximal affine regions of h h . In the second, 𝒯 \mathcal{T} ranges over conforming triangulations of I I with at most N N vertices, and ℐ 𝒯 ​ q \mathcal{I}_{\mathcal{T}}q interpolates q q at those nodes. The affine regions of h h can be triangulated without adding vertices, using a pulling triangulation ( De Loera et al., 2010 , Section 4.3.2) . On each simplex T = conv ⁡ { v 1 , … , v k } T=\conv\{v_{1},\ldots,v_{k}\} , where k = m + 1 k=m+1 , write x = ∑ i λ i ​ ( x ) ​ v i x=\sum_{i}\lambda_{i}(x)v_{i} , with λ i ​ ( x ) ≥ 0 \lambda_{i}(x)\geq 0 and ∑ i λ i ​ ( x ) = 1 \sum_{i}\lambda_{i}(x)=1 . Lemma 6 below gives the interpolation gap W T ​ ( x ) := ℐ T ​ q ​ ( x ) − q ⁡ ( x ) = ∑ i < j | v i − v j | 2 ​ λ i ​ ( x ) ​ λ j ​ ( x ) . W_{T}(x):=\mathcal{I}_{T}q(x)-q(x)=\sum_{i<j}|v_{i}-v_{j}|^{2}\lambda_{i}(x)\lambda_{j}(x). Changing the vertex heights adds an affine function, so h − q = W T + L T , L T ​ ( x ) = ∑ i d i ​ λ i ​ ( x ) , d i = h ⁡ ( v i ) − q ⁡ ( v i ) . h-q=W_{T}+L_{T},\qquad L_{T}(x)=\sum_{i}d_{i}\lambda_{i}(x),\qquad d_{i}=h(v_{i})-q(v_{i}). We therefore want a lower bound that holds for every choice of the coefficients d i d_{i} . An inspiration for the sign construction is the classical dual formula for L 1 L^{1} approximation ( Pinkus, 1989 , Chapter 2) . If A ⁡ ( T ) A(T) is the space of affine functions on T T , then for every real f ∈ L 1 ​ ( T ) f\in L^{1}(T) , inf ℓ ∈ A ⁡ ( T ) ∫ T | f − ℓ | ​ 𝑑 x = sup σ ∈ L ∞ ​ ( T ) , ‖ σ ‖ ∞ ≤ 1 ∫ T σ ​ ℓ ​ d x = 0 ​ for every ​ ℓ ∈ A ⁡ ( T ) ∫ T σ ​ f ​ 𝑑 x . \addcontentsline{lla}{section}{
umberline q:intro-dual-L1}\inf_{\ell\in A(T)}\int_{T}|f-\ell|\,dx=\sup_{ egin{subarray}{c}\sigma\in L^{\infty}(T),\ \|\sigma\|_{\infty}\leq 1\ \int_{T}\sigma\ell\,dx=0\ 	ext{for every }\ell\in A(T)\end{subarray}}\int_{T}\sigma f\,dx. (35) This formula suggests looking for a bounded function whose integral against every affine correction is zero. A related geometric example also appears in harmonic approximation ( Khavinson et al., 2000 , Section 6) . Here we choose a sign by making a similar balanced cut in barycentric coordinates. Let X X be uniform on T T . Its barycentric vector Λ = ( λ 1 ​ ( X ) , … , λ k ​ ( X ) ) \Lambda=(\lambda_{1}(X),\ldots,\lambda_{k}(X)) is uniform on the standard simplex, or equivalently has the Dirichlet k ⁡ ( 1 , … , 1 ) \operatorname{Dirichlet}_{k}(1,\ldots,1) distribution. Define Q k = ∑ i Λ i 2 , μ k = med ⁡ ( Q k ) , σ k = sign ⁡ ( μ k − Q k ) . Q_{k}=\sum_{i}\Lambda_{i}^{2},\qquad\mu_{k}=\operatorname{med}(Q_{k}),\qquad\sigma_{k}=\operatorname{sign}(\mu_{k}-Q_{k}). The statistic Q k Q_{k} is small near the centroid and large near the vertices. Its level sets have zero volume, so the median divides the barycentric simplex into two equal-volume parts. Thus σ k \sigma_{k} takes the value + 1 +1 on the central half and − 1 -1 on the outer half, giving 𝔼 ​ σ k = 0 \mathbb{E}\sigma_{k}=0 . On a regular simplex, W T W_{T} is proportional to 1 − Q k 1-Q_{k} , so this is also the usual sign of the error after subtracting the best constant, which is a median. The sign and the uniform barycentric distribution are unchanged by permutations of the coordinates. Hence all 𝔼 ⁡ ( σ k ​ Λ i ) \mathbb{E}(\sigma_{k}\Lambda_{i}) are equal. Since ∑ i Λ i = 1 \sum_{i}\Lambda_{i}=1 , 𝔼 ⁡ ( σ k ​ Λ i ) = 1 k ​ 𝔼 ​ ( σ k ​ ∑ j Λ j ) = 0 . \mathbb{E}(\sigma_{k}\Lambda_{i})= rac{1}{k}\mathbb{E}\left(\sigma_{k}\sum_{j}\Lambda_{j}ight)=0. It follows that 𝔼 ⁡ [ σ k ​ L T ​ ( X ) ] = 0 \mathbb{E}[\sigma_{k}L_{T}(X)]=0 , whatever the vertex heights are. This argument works on every simplex: the symmetry is in its barycentric coordinates, rather than its Euclidean shape. The same symmetry lets us calculate the contribution of W T W_{T} . All 𝔼 ⁡ ( σ k ​ Λ i ​ Λ j ) \mathbb{E}(\sigma_{k}\Lambda_{i}\Lambda_{j}) with i ≠ j i
