The annihilation principle from Section 7.3 also applies when the number of facets is prescribed. We again construct a bounded sign that erases every affine correction and retains a fixed fraction of a quadratic error. Here the local domains are convex cells, and a homothetic copy about the centroid supplies the required moment identities. Keep q ⁡ ( x ) = | x | 2 q(x)=|x|^{2} and I = [ 0 , 1 ] m I=[0,1]^{m} . The facet analogues of ( 94 )–( 95 ) are e N div ​ ( I ) \displaystyle e_{N}^{\operatorname{div}}(I) : = inf a 1 , … , a N ∈ ℝ m ∫ I min 1 ≤ i ≤ N ⁡ | x − a i | 2 ​ 𝑑 x , \displaystyle:=\inf_{a_{1},\ldots,a_{N}\in\mathbb{R}^{m}}\int_{I}\min_{1\leq i\leq N}|x-a_{i}|^{2}\,dx, (160) e N ldiv ​ ( I ) \displaystyle e_{N}^{\ldiv}(I) : = inf h : I → ℝ ​ convex, continuous, piecewise affine # ​ 𝒜 ​ ( h ) ≤ N ∫ I | h − q | d x , \displaystyle:=\inf_{ egin{subarray}{c}h:I	o\mathbb{R}	ext{ convex, continuous, piecewise affine}\ \#\mathcal{A}(h)\leq N\end{subarray}}\int_{I}|h-q|\,dx, (161) where 𝒜 ⁡ ( h ) \mathcal{A}(h) is the collection of maximal full-dimensional affine regions of h h , clipped to I I . Thus, the budget counts affine regions, each corresponding to one active facet of the graph. The first error corresponds to approximation of q q by tangent supporting planes, while the second allows arbitrary affine planes. The local characterization of div m \operatorname{div}_{m} ( Gruber, 1993b ) and Ludwig’s corresponding characterization of ldiv m \operatorname{ldiv}_{m} ( Ludwig, 1999 , Section 2, Lemma 1) give us div m = lim N → ∞ N 2 / m ​ e N div ​ ( I ) and ldiv m = lim N → ∞ N 2 / m ​ e N ldiv ​ ( I ) . \addcontentsline{lla}{section}{
