ε \varepsilon -accuracy requires at least exp ⁡ ( c ⁡ ( ε ) ​ d ) \exp(c(\varepsilon)d) vertices. Consequently, the Euclidean ball does not maximize the optimal relative approximation error among convex bodies, even up to an absolute multiplicative constant at the same vertex budget. Thus Macbeath’s theorem ( Macbeath, 1951 ) , which establishes the extremality of the ball for approximation by inscribed polytopes, does not extend to polytopes in arbitrary position. † † 2020 Mathematics Subject Classification : Primary 52A27; Secondary 52A23, 52A22. † † Key words and phrases : Polytope approximation, Euclidean ball, random polytopes, Laguerre–Delone triangulation number, symmetric difference metric, surface area deviation. 1 Introduction Let 𝒫 d ​ ( N ) \mathcal{P}_{d}(N) and 𝒫 ¯ d ​ ( M ) \overline{\mathcal{P}}_{d}(M) denote the classes of polytopes in ℝ d \mathbb{R}^{d} , d ≥ 2 d\geq 2 , with at most N N vertices and at most M M facets, respectively. Unless explicitly stated, we impose no containment restriction. For a polytope P P , define its relative symmetric difference from the Euclidean unit ball by Δ d ​ ( P ) := vol d ⁡ ( B 2 d ​ △ ​ P ) vol d ⁡ ( B 2 d ) . \addcontentsline{lla}{section}{
